# EXPECT_RUNTIME_ERROR
#~
A C++ exception thrown inside a call the JIT's bridge made ends the program
with the VM's internal-error line and exit status 1, not with a process abort.

Neither backend registers unwind information for the code it emits, so an
exception thrown in a bridge call -- by the interpreter the bridge nests for a
callee with no native code, by a trap, or by the bridge's own switch -- could
not pass through the compiled frame below it: libc++ terminated the process
(SIGABRT, exit 134, "terminating due to uncaught exception") before Execute's
catch in vm.cpp printed anything, and libstdc++ and Windows end the same way.
The bridge's two entries catch the exception now and report it as Execute
would have: the same ">>> virtual machine: internal error: ... <<<" line, the
stack, exit(1).

The regression runner passes an expected runtime error on any non-zero exit
with output, so it cannot tell the abort from the error; run_vm_flag_tests.py
(section 6) names the status and the message, and this file keeps the shape
in both regression passes. Parse is native, so it is compiled in the plain
pass too, and String->ToFloat runs under the bridge either way: in the plain
pass inside the interpreter the bridge nests (a library method called once
stays interpreted), and with every method compiled in the bridge's own S2F
case. Both routes end in the same catch.

The way in is "1e999"->ToFloat(): std::stod throws std::out_of_range, which
neither the interpreter's Str2Float nor the bridge's S2F catches, on every
platform. Nothing in the VM throws on purpose, so a fixture for this catch
rides on an unguarded throw; guard that one and this file needs another way
in, which the runner reports as "should have failed at runtime".
~#

class JitBridgeException {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		"before"->PrintLine();
		Parse("1e999")->PrintLine();
		"after"->PrintLine();
	}

	function : native : Parse(s : String) ~ Float {
		return s->ToFloat();
	}
}
